            Last Minute News on B-Tree Filer Version 5.41
            =============================================

This file describes changes, additions, and clarifications to B-Tree
Filer that do not appear in the printed documentation. Please print
and read this file prior to using B-Tree Filer, and save it for future
reference. It includes the following sections:

    1.  Compiler Version Support
    2.  DOS Protected Mode and Windows Support
    3.  Conditional Defines and Compiler Directives
    4.  FILER
    5.  EMSHEAP
    6.  ISAMTOOL
    7.  NETWARE
    8.  VREC
    9.  DBIMPEXP
   10.  BROWSER
   11.  MSORT
   12.  NUMKEYS
   13.  Clarifications on Filer Network usage
   14.  NetWare Shell Warning
   15.  Corrections to the Manual

Subscribers to TurboPower's Fast Update Plan may wish to read the file
FASTUPD.* (where * is the most recent version number, e.g.,
FASTUPD.541), which summarizes the changes made during the most recent
version update.

We have now released the third edition of the B-Tree Filer manual. It
incorporates all known corrections and enhancements contained in Filer
versions up through 5.23, with the exception of notes regarding
support for Microsoft Windows, which are still found in this file. If
you are receiving this version of B-Tree Filer as an update and would
like to purchase a copy of the third edition manual, you may do so for
$25. Add $5 for airmail shipping within the U.S. and Canada, or $10
elsewhere. Please supply your serial number when ordering. Call
TurboPower at 800-333-4160 (U.S. and Canada), 719-260-9136
(elsewhere), or 719-260-7151 (fax).


1.  Compiler Version Support
=====================================================================
As of version 5.40, B-Tree Filer can be used with any of the following
versions of Turbo Pascal:

   Turbo Pascal 5.0
   Turbo Pascal 5.5
   Turbo Pascal 6.0
   Turbo Pascal 7.0
   Borland Pascal 7.0
   Turbo Pascal for Windows 1.0
   Turbo Pascal for Windows 1.5

When using Borland Pascal 7.0, B-Tree Filer can be compiled for real
mode, DOS protected mode, or Windows targets.

The B-Tree Filer units compensate internally for different compiler
versions and target environments by the use of conditionally defined
code. Conditional logic and defines in the BTDEFINE.INC file are used
to get the appropriate behavior. If you have upgraded to this version
of B-Tree Filer from an earlier version, be certain that you are using
the most recent version of BTDEFINE.INC, or you will run into
compilation problems.

The operation of the database-related units (FILER, VREC, REORG,
REBUILD, VREORG, VREBUILD, FIXTOVAR, ISAMTOOL) is identical regardless
of the target environment. Only the network-specific units NETWARE and
NETBIOS are used differently in DOS real mode versus DOS protected
mode or Windows. See the following section for differences in the
various target environments.


2.  DOS Protected Mode and Windows Support
=====================================================================
With one exception, all production units of B-Tree Filer are now
compatible with DOS real mode, DOS protected mode, and Windows
operation. The one exception is the BROWSER unit, which is not
compatible with Windows. However, a Windows-compatible file browser is
provided as a BONUS unit: see OBROWSE.LZH on the BONUS disk. This
archive contains documentation for the browser as well as sample
applications based on the browser and Borland's ObjectWindows Library.

The following subsections describe issues that arise when using
various Filer units under Windows or DOS protected mode. DOS protected
mode is abbreviated as DPMI in the following.

FILER
-----
A new unit BASESUPP is used internally to route MsDos calls to the
appropriate DOS, DPMI, or Windows routines.

Due to the increased amount of heap space available to Windows or DPMI
programs, you must rethink the memory-related parameters you pass to
<BTInitIsam>. If, for example, you passed the value 0 for the Free
parameter and there were 10 megabytes of free heap space (some of it
possibly provided by virtual memory disk space), then <BTInitIsam>
would spend an extremely long time allocating all of this space and
you wouldn't end up with significantly improved B-Tree performance
anyway.

Under Windows, it's generally best to ask <BTInitIsam> for a fixed
size page buffer, as shown in the following example:

  Pages := BTInitIsam(WhateverNet, MemAvail-500000, 0);

This call would allocate 500,000 bytes of heap space for the page
buffers.

EMS is not supported under either Windows or DPMI, so you should pass
0 for the last parameter to BTInitIsam in this situation, however the
parameter will be ignored if you do not.

When running multiple FILER-based tasks under Windows, it's best to
use the MsNet network model. SHARE.EXE (provided with DOS) must be
loaded before starting Windows. FILER automatically gives each window
a unique "workstation number" when it opens a fileblock already opened
by another window.

NETWARE
-------
In order to make calls to the Novell NetWare API while running under
Windows or DPMI, the NETWARE unit must be able to allocate memory for
use in real mode (memory in the first megabyte of address space). This
real mode memory is a limited resource. Please see the discussion of
real mode memory in the documentation for the WINDPMI unit, later in
this file.

In addition to the NETWARE unit itself, the NETSEMA unit and the two
BONUS units NETBIND and NETQUE have also been modified to support
Windows and DPMI. There have been no changes to the interface or
calling sequence of any of the routines in those BONUS units.

The routines in the NETWARE unit will not work with Windows' Real
mode. They are compatible with both Windows Standard and 386 Enhanced
mode, as well as DPMI.

To use Novell's NetWare and its associated API calls with Windows, you
will need Windows 3.0a or greater. NetWare does not work well with
Windows 3.00. Novell recommends a minimum version of IPX 3.04 and
NetWare shell version 3.22 or greater. These versions can be
downloaded from CompuServe or obtained from Novell.

The following example programs that use the NETWARE unit have been
updated to compile and run under real mode DOS, DPMI, and Windows:

  NETINFO
  MESEXAMP

The sample programs NSEND and NRECEIVE, required many changes since
they rely heavily on network transfer protocols. Versions of these
programs that run under Windows or DPMI have been provided in the
files NSENDW.PAS and NRECEIVW.PAS.

The following new types, constants and variables have been added when
compiling NETWARE for Windows or DPMI:

const
  DPMIErrorVal  = $AA;   {returned when a DPMI service request failed}

For functions in the NetWare unit that return a byte error code, the
new error code $AA will be returned for errors caused by failure of a
DPMI call (usually due to insufficient real mode memory).

type
  Registers = TRegisters;

This type is defined to save us the trouble of changing every routine
using the Registers type, which has been renamed to TRegisters in
TPW's WinDos unit.

var
  NoESR : UserESRType absolute NilPtr;

This is a predefined do-nothing Event Service Routine.

There is no change to the interface of any routines in the NETWARE
unit except the IPX and SPX calls. A discussion of the changes to IPX
and SPX routines follows.

IPX/SPX
-------
When using the IPX and/or SPX routines in the NETWARE unit under
Windows or DPMI, an additional DLL called IPXSPXD.DLL is used. The
source to this DLL is contained within the source file IPXSPXD.PAS.
Windows or the Borland DPMI runtime manager will load the DLL if and
when it needs to, provided IPXSPXD.DLL is in the Windows directory,
the Windows System directory, the current directory, or on the DOS
path. Your program does not need to reference this DLL, since the
NETWARE unit knows how to load and use it.

The following types have been added to support IPX/SPX under Windows
and DPMI:

  WinIPXRec =
    record
      wirP, wirR : IPXRecPtr;
    end;

This record type replaces the IPXRec in most IPX calls. It contains
both real and protected mode pointers to an IPXRec. A variable of this
type is initialized with a call to AllocateWinIPXRec and disposed with
a call to FreeWinIPXRec.

  AESECBPtr = ^AESECB;

A pointer to an AESECB.

  WinAESECB =
    record
      AESECBR, AESECBP : AESECBPtr;
    end;

This record type replaces the AESECB in IPXScheduleEvent and
IPXScheduleSpecialEvent. It contains both real and protected mode
pointers to an AESECB. A variable of this type is initialized with a
call to AllocateWinAESECB and disposed of with a call to
FreeWinAESECB.

  WinSPXPoolRec =
    record
      prR, prP : SPXPoolRecPtr;
    end;

This record type replaces the SPXPoolRec in certain SPX calls. It
contains both real and protected mode pointers to an SPXPoolRec. This
is a component of the WinSPXRec. The WinSPXPoolRec is allocated and
maintained when you call SPXEstablishConn or SPXListenForConn. You
need to pass a WinSPXPoolRec directly to certain pool management SPX
routines. Assuming we have a WinSPXRec called Event, the following
code shows how you would pass the WinSPXPoolRec field to a call to
SPXPoolCancelAll:

  SPXPoolCancelAll(Event.wsrP^.PoolRec);


  WinSPXRec =
    record
      wsrR, wsrP : SPXRecPtr;
    end;

This record type replaces the SPXRec in most SPX calls. It contains
both real and protected mode pointers to an SPXRec. A variable of this
type is initialized with a call to AllocateWinSPXRec and disposed of
with a call to FreeWinSPXRec.

  UserESRType = procedure (var SPXEvent : WinSPXRec;
                           CompletionCode : Byte;
                           PoolIndex : Byte;
                           DataType : Byte;
                           DataPtr : Pointer);

This is the procedure type used for event service routines written in
TPW or DPMI.

  WinESRType =
    record
      Regs     : DPMIReg;
      PoolPtr  : SPXPoolRecPtr;
      CallBack : Pointer;
      UserESR  : UserESRType;
      DataSegm : Word;
    end;

This record structure contains information needed to allow
applications running under Windows or DPMI to have SPX Event Service
Routines. A procedure of type UserESRType is associated with a
WinESRType variable through a call to AllocateWindowsESR.

The following new routines have been added to the NETWARE unit:

function AllocateWinIPXRec(DataSize : Word;
                           var Event : WinIPXRec) : Boolean;
  {-Allocates a WinIPXRec which contains pointers to an IPXRec
    addressable in both real and protected modes. Also allocates space
    for the data addressable in real mode. You must call these
    routines to initialize a variable of type WinIPXRec before passing
    that variable to the other IPX routines.}

procedure FreeWinIPXRec(Event : WinIPXRec);
  {-Frees a WinIPXRec previously allocated with AllocateWinIPXRec. The
    event associated with this WinIPXRec must be completed or
    cancelled before this procedure is called.}

function AllocateWinSPXRec(var WSR : WinSPXRec) : Boolean;
  {-Allocates a WinSPXRec which contains pointers to an SPXRec
    addressable in both real and protected modes. Also allocates space
    for the data addressable in real mode. You must call these
    routines to initialize a variable of type WinSPXRec before passing
    that variable to the other SPX routines.}

function AllocateWinAESECB(DataSize : Word;
                           var ECB : WinAESECB) : Boolean;
  {-Allocates a WinAESECB which contains pointers to an AESECB
    addressable in both real and protected modes. Also allocates space
    for the data addressable in real mode.}

procedure FreeWinAESECB(ECB : WinAESECB);
  {-Frees a WinAESECB previously allocated with AllocateWinAESECB. The
    event associated with this WinAESECB must be completed or
    cancelled before this procedure is called.}

procedure FreeWinSPXRec(WSR : WinSPXRec);
  {-Frees a WinSPXRec previously allocated with AllocateWinSPXRec. The
    event associated with this WinSPXRec must be completed or
    cancelled before this procedure is called.}

function AllocateWindowsESR(EventServiceRoutine : UserESRType;
                            DataSegment : Word;
                            var WindowsESR : WinESRType) : Boolean;
  {-Requests a real mode callback and initializes the data structures
    needed to support ESRs in WinApps.}

procedure FreeWindowsESR(var WindowsESR : WinESRType);
  {-Free a real mode callback previously allocated with a call to
    AllocateWindowsESR.}

The interface to many of the existing IPX/SPX routines has been
modified to support Windows and DPMI. The following list shows each
changed high level routine showing the new parameters:

function IPXCancelEvent(IPXEvent : WinIPXRec) : Byte;

function IPXEventComplete(IPXEvent : WinIPXRec;
                          var FinalResult : Byte) : Boolean;

function IPXListen(IPXEvent : WinIPXRec;
                   Socket : Word;
                   WaitForCompletion : Boolean;
                   MaxPacketSize : Word;
                   var DataPacket) : Byte;

function IPXSend(IPXEvent : WinIPXRec;
                 Receiver : IPXAddress;
                 Socket : Word;
                 WaitForCompletion : Boolean;
                 DataPacketSize : Word;
                 var DataPacket) : Byte;

procedure IPXScheduleEvent(DelayTime : Word; ECB : WinIPXECB);

procedure IPXScheduleSpecialEvent(DelayTime : Word;
                                  ECB : WinAESECB);

procedure SPXAbortConn(SPXEvent : WinSPXRec);

function SPXEstablishConn(var SPXEvent : WinSPXRec;
                          Receiver : IPXAddress;
                          Socket : Word;
                          WaitForCompletion : Boolean;
                          MaxListenDataSize : Word;
                          NumListenPoolECBs : Byte;
                          PoolESRInPascal : Pointer) : Byte;

function SPXEventComplete(SPXEvent : WinSPXRec;
                          var FinalResult : Byte;
                          var DataType : Byte) : Boolean;

function SPXListenForConn(var SPXEvent : WinSPXRec;
                          Socket : Word;
                          WaitForCompletion : Boolean;
                          MaxDataSize : Word;
                          NumPoolECBs : Byte;
                          PoolESRInPascal : Pointer) : Byte;

procedure SPXPoolStartListening(var PoolRec : WinSPXPoolRec);

function SPXListenCount(SPXEvent : WinSPXRec) : Byte;

function SPXListenPooled(SPXEvent : WinSPXRec;
                         var CompleteCode : Byte;
                         var PoolIndex : Byte;
                         var DataType : Byte;
                         var DataPtr : Pointer) : Boolean;

function SPXPoolCancelAll(PoolRec : WinSPXPoolRec) : Boolean;

function SPXPoolECBPtr(PoolRec : WinSPXPoolRec;
                       PoolIndex : Byte) : ECBPtr;

function SPXPoolDataPtr(PoolRec : WinSPXPoolRec;
                        PoolIndex : Byte) : Pointer;

procedure SPXReplenishPool(SPXEvent : WinSPXRec; PoolIndex : Byte);

procedure SPXReplenishAll(SPXEvent : WinSPXRec);

procedure SPXSend(SPXEvent : WinSPXRec;
                  WaitForCompletion : Boolean;
                  DataType : Byte;
                  DataPacketSize : Word;
                  DataPacket : Pointer);

procedure SPXTerminateConn(SPXEvent : WinSPXRec);

Many of the IPX/SPX routines include a WaitForCompletion option,
meaning that the procedure will not wait for completion of the
requested event. When WaitForCompletion is False, the WinIPXRec or
WinSPXRec must reside in static memory for the life of the event. In
other words, you couldn't have a local variable for the WinIPXRec or
WinSPXRec unless you could assure that the procedure in which that
variable was declared was not exited until the event was complete.
Windows and DPMI add an additional complication to this rule. Since
NetWare is implemented as a TSR running in real mode underneath
Windows or DPMI, the memory must not only be static for the life of
the call, but it must be addressable in both real and protected mode.
The new routines AllocateWinIPXRec and AllocateWinSPXRec deal with
this issue.

AllocateWinIPXRec and AllocateWinSPXRec are used to allocate memory
for the event record addressable in both real and protected mode, and
FreeWinIPXRec and FreeSPXRec are used to free that memory. It is very
important to free the memory allocated with these routines by calling
FreeWinIPXRec or FreeWinSPXRec when you are finished with a given
event record. At the same time, it is critical that you do not free
that memory until the event associated with that event record has been
completed (or explicitly cancelled).

SPX Event Service Routines
--------------------------
Upon completion of an SPX event, a user provided routine can be called
automatically. This routine is called an Event Service Routine (ESR).
When compiling under TPW, you must first declare a variable of type
WinESRType. Then this variable must be initialized using
AllocateWindowsESR. This WinESR variable is then passed to
SPXEstablishConn or SPXListenForConn. When an SPX packet is received
by one of the ECBs in the listening pool, the routine passed to
AllocateWindowsESR will be called.

The following code fragment shows the declaration of a Windows ESR and
the calls needed to use it:

var
  ListenESR : WinESRType;

  procedure OurESR(var Event : WinSPXRec; CompletionCode : Byte;
                   PoolIndex : Byte; DataType : Byte;
                   DataPtr : Pointer); Far;
  begin
  end;

begin
  ...
  if not AllocateWindowsESR(OurESR, DSeg, ListenESR) then
    Abort('Unable to allocate real mode callback');
  Ret := SPXListenForConn(Event, LSocket, False, DataSize,
                          NumECBs, ListenESR);

Note the declaration of OurESR. Your ESR must have identical
parameters, and it must be a FAR, global procedure.

Even if you do not require your own ESR, you must still declare a
variable of type WinESR and make a call to AllocateWindowsESR. For the
EventServiceRoutine parameter, specify the predefined variable NoESR.

The reason you must declare and allocate a WinESR even if you do not
need an ESR is because the NETWARE unit needs to use the ESR to manage
the ECB pools. Under DOS, this is handled transparently. Under
Windows, data contained within the WinESR and initialized by
AllocateWindowsESR is critical to the operation of the ESR.

Note: A given WinESR variable may be used with only one call to
SPXEstablishConn or SPXListenForConn at a time. This is because the
WinESR contains internal pointers back to the associated WinSPXEvent.
This is necessary to allow the ESR to "find its way" back from real
mode into protected mode and call the appropriate protected mode Event
Service Routine.

When your ESR is called, Windows is in a critical section. While in a
critical section, Windows cannot multitask. Therefore you should exit
your ESR as quickly as possible. (Because DOS DPMI is not
multi-tasking, this issue is not so important under DPMI.
Nevertheless, the ESR is an event service routine and it should exit
as quickly as possible.)

When you are finished with a variable of type WinESR, it is important
to dispose of it with a call to FreeWindowsESR. You must be careful to
ensure the events associated with the ESR are really complete or have
been explicitly cancelled. If you call FreeWindowsESR while the
associated ESR is still active, you will get a DPMI or Windows UAE or
system hang if the ESR is called.

NETBIOS
-------
When compiled under Windows or DPMI, the NETBIOS unit requires a DLL
called NETBIOSD.DLL. The source to NETBIOSD.DLL is implemented in
NETBIOSD.PAS. Windows or the Borland DPMI runtime manager will load
the DLL if and when it needs to, provided NETBIOSD.DLL is in the
Windows directory, the Windows System directory, the current
directory, or on the DOS path. Your program does not need to reference
this DLL, since the NETBIOS unit knows how to load and use it.

In order to make calls to the NetBIOS API, the NETBIOS unit must be
able to allocate memory for use in real mode (memory in the first
megabyte of address space). This real mode memory is a limited
resource. Please see the discussion of real mode memory in the
documentation for the WINDPMI unit.

The routines in the NETBIOS unit will not work in Windows Real mode.

The following example programs that use the NETBIOS unit have been
updated to compile and run under both DOS, DPMI, and Windows:

  NETINFO
  MESEXAMP

The sample programs NSEND and NRECEIVE, required many changes since
they rely heavily on network transfer protocols. Versions of these
programs for DPMI or Windows have been provided in the files
NSENDW.PAS and NRECEIVW.PAS.

The following new types have been added when compiling with TPW:

  WinNCB =
    record
      NR, NP : NCBPtr;
    end;

This record type holds both real and protected mode pointers to an
NCB. A variable of this type must be initialized with AllocateWinNCB
and disposed with FreeWinNCB.

  WinNCBPtr = ^WinNCB;

A pointer to a WinNCB.

  NetBiosPostRoutine = procedure(LastError : Byte; N : WinNCBPtr);

This procedure type defines NetBIOS post routines for use in Windows
applications.

  WinPostType =
    record
      Regs     : DPMIReg;
      Post     : NetBiosPostRoutine;
      CallBack : RealModeCallbackProc;
      NCBs     : WinNCBPtr;
    end;

This record holds various information needed for the NETBIOS unit to
allow NetBIOS post routines while running under Windows. A variable of
this type is passed to all the NetBIOS functions with NoWait options.
A variable of this type must be initialized with a call to
GetWindowsPostRoutine or filled with zeros if no post routine is
desired. When you have finished with it, it is disposed with a call to
FreeWindowsPostRoutine.

The following new routines have been added. The use of these routines
will be explained later in the text.

function GetWindowsPostRoutine(ProtectedProc : NetBiosPostRoutine;
                               DataSegment : Word;
                               var WinPost : WinPostType) : Boolean;
  {-Request a real mode callback for NetBIOS post routines}

procedure FreeWindowsPostRoutine(var WinPost : WinPostType);
  {-Free a real mode callback for NetBIOS post routines}

function AllocateWinNCB(var N : WinNCB) : Boolean;
  {-Allocate memory for NCB for use under Windows}

procedure FreeWinNCB(N : WinNCB);
  {-Free memory for NCB for use under Windows}

When compiling under TPW, the following routines have a slightly
different interface than their DOS counterparts:

function NetBiosSend(SessionLSN : Byte;
                     PacketSize : Word;
                     Packet : Pointer) : Byte;

function NetBiosReceive(SessionLSN : Byte;
                        PacketSize : Word;
                        Packet : Pointer) : Byte;

procedure NetBiosReceiveNoWait(SessionLSN : Byte;
                               PacketSize : Word;
                               var PostEvent  : WinPostType;
                               var N : WinNCB;
                               Packet : Pointer);

procedure NetBiosSendNoWait(SessionLSN : Byte;
                            PacketSize : Word;
                            var PostEvent  : WinPostType;
                            var N : WinNCB;
                            Packet : Pointer);

function NetBiosCmdCompleted(var N : WinNCB;
                             var FinalRetCode : Byte) : Boolean;

function CancelRequest(var N : WinNCB) : Byte;

procedure SendDataGram(var N : WinNCB; SenderNameNum : Byte;
                       ReceiverName : NBNameStr;
                       Wait : Boolean;
                       var PostEvent : WinPostType;
                       DGSize : Word;
                       Datagram : Pointer);

procedure ReceiveDataGram(var N : WinNCB;
                          ReceiverNameNum : Byte;
                          Wait : Boolean;
                          var PostEvent : WinPostType;
                          DGSize : Word;
                          Datagram : Pointer);

procedure SendBDataGram(var N : WinNCB; SenderNameNum : Byte;
                        Wait : Boolean;
                        var PostEvent : WinPostType;
                        DGSize : Word;
                        Datagram : Pointer);

procedure ReceiveBDataGram(var N : WinNCB;
                           ReceiverNameNum : Byte;
                           Wait : Boolean;
                           var PostEvent : WinPostType;
                           DGSize : Word;
                           Datagram : Pointer);

procedure NetBiosCallNoWait(RemoteName,LocalName : NBNameStr;
                            SendTimeOut,RecTimeOut : Byte;
                            var PostEvent : WinPostType;
                            var N : WinNCB);

procedure NetBiosListenNoWait(RemoteName,LocalName : NBNameStr;
                              SendTimeOut,RecTimeOut : Byte;
                              var PostEvent : WinPostType;
                              var N : WinNCB);

procedure NetBIOSRequest(N : NCBPtr);

Many of the NetBIOS routines include Wait options, meaning that the
procedure may not wait for completion of the requested event. When
Wait is False, the NCB must reside in static memory for the life of
the event. In other words, you couldn't have a local variable for the
NCB unless you could assure that the procedure in which that variable
was declared was not exited until the event was complete. DPMI and
Windows add an additional complication to this rule. Since NetBIOS is
implemented as a TSR or device driver running in real mode underneath
Windows or DPMI, the memory must also be addressable in both real and
protected mode. The new routines AllocateWinNCB and FreeWinNCB deal
with this issue.

AllocateWinNCB is used to allocate NCB memory addressable in both real
and protected mode, and FreeWinNCB is used to free that memory. It is
very important to free the memory allocated with AllocateWinNCB by
calling FreeWinNCB when you are finished with a given NCB. At the same
time, it is critical that you do not free that memory until the event
associated with that NCB has been completed (or explicitly cancelled).

One subtle but important change has been made to all the functions
that take data packets. In the DOS versions of these routines, the
parameters Packet and Datagram are untyped variables. In the Windows
version they are pointers. This change was not made simply to annoy
you! Because NetBIOS is running in real mode underneath Windows, it
expects all pointers it receives in function calls to be real mode
pointers. Since the Windows or DPMI application is running in
protected mode, it has to use special techniques to make real mode
memory available. You must use the routine GetRealModeMem (actually a
function pointer interfaced by the WINDPMI unit-see below) to allocate
memory that can be addressed by both real and protected mode
processes. GetRealModeMem returns two pointers, one usable in real
mode, the other in protected mode. When referencing the memory from
your Windows application, you use the protected mode pointer
exclusively. The only time you use the real mode pointer is when
making a call to any NetBIOS function. The following example should
help clarify:

uses
  WinDPMI, NetBIOS;

var
  SR, SP : ^String;
  ...

begin
  if not GetRealModeMem(SizeOf(String), Pointer(SR), Pointer(SP)) then
  begin
    WriteLn('Insufficient real mode memory.');
    Halt;
  end;
  SP^ := 'This pointer has been assigned in protected mode, for real!';

  {assume SendN, NameNo, NBName, and SendPost are initialized}
  SendDatagram(SendN, NameNo, NBName, False, SendPost,
               SizeOf(String), SR);

  {assure event completes}
  FreeRealModeMem(Pointer(SP));

In this example, we allocate the memory for SR and SP with
GetRealModeMem. Then we initialize the string using the protected mode
pointer SP. Finally, we make the call to SendDatagram to send this
string, passing the real mode pointer. This issue may seem confusing,
but the rule is simple: when using a pointer in your program, use the
protected mode pointer, when passing the pointer to NetBIOS, pass the
real mode pointer. If you get this wrong, you will get either an UAE
or a bogus message sent via NetBIOS. Study the NBCHATW program found
in the BONUS directory for more examples of using real and protected
mode pointers.

You will notice that all the NetBIOS routines that have a NoWait
option take a parameter of type WinNCB. WinNCB is a record type
containing both a real mode and a protected mode pointer to a block of
memory addressable in both modes. This memory is allocated by
AllocateWinNCB.

See the sample program NBCHATW.PAS for an example of how
AllocateWinNCB and FreeWinNCB are used in a Windows application.

The NETBIOS unit supports NetBIOS Post routines for the NoWait
procedures. Post routines are routines that are called automatically
upon completion of the associated event. Again Windows and DPMI
complicate matters because NetBIOS expects the post routines to be
real mode procedures, but the Windows application is running in
protected mode (if Windows is in Standard or 386 Enhanced mode). Two
routines have been added to deal with this: GetWindowsPostRoutine and
FreeWindowsPostRoutine.

To use the NoWait NETBIOS routines, you need to declare a variable of
type WinPostType. Before making the call to the NoWait routine, you
must first either use GetWindowsPostRoutine to initialize the
WinPostType or you must initialize the WinPostType to 0 with FillChar
to indicate no post routine is desired. When calling
GetWindowsPostRoutine, you must provide a variable of type WinPostType
and a procedural parameter of type NetBiosPostRoutine. A
NetBiosPostRoutine is a FAR, global procedure declared as follows:

  procedure MyPostRoutine(LastError : Byte; N : WinNCBPtr); Far;

The name of the procedure is not important (you may use any name you
like), but the parameters must exactly match those shown. TPW or BP7
will generate a compiler error on the call to GetWindowsPostRoutine if
the post routine is declared incorrectly.

Also passed to GetWindowsPostRoutine is a DataSegment value. This is
the protected mode data segment selector that will be put in the DS
register just before your post routine is activated. Typically you
should pass DSeg for this parameter.

Note: A given variable of type WinPostType may be associated with only
one active NetBIOS call at a time. This is because the WinPostType has
a field used internally that points back to the NCB for the associated
event.

When your Post routine is called, Windows is in a critical section.
While in a critical section, Windows cannot multitask. Therefore you
should exit your Post routine as quickly as possible. (Because DOS
DPMI is not multi-tasking, this issue is not so important under DPMI.
Nevertheless, the post routine is an event service routine and it
should exit as quickly as possible.)

When the NetBIOS event associated with a given post routine completes,
your procedure will be called automatically. It receives as parameters
the final error code for the event in LastError, and a pointer to the
WinNCB in N. A typical NetBIOS post routine will queue the data for
processing and resubmit the NCB for listening. The following post
routine is taken from the one in the NBCHATW program:

  procedure PostRoutine(LastError : Byte; N : WinNCBPtr); Far;
  begin
    if Exiting then Exit;
    Pending := True;
    if LastError = 0 then
      Msg := SP^
    else
      Msg := 'NetBIOS error = ' + HexB(LastError) + 'h';
    ReceiveDatagram(N^, NBNameNo, False, Post, SizeOf(String), SR);
  end;

First, it checks the global variable Exiting to see if the program has
begun shutting itself down. If it is shutting down, the post routine
exits immediately. If not, it sets the global flag Pending to true to
inform the main message loop that an incoming string is waiting in the
string variable Msg. If no error occured, the incoming string is taken
from the data buffer (in this case a string pointer called SP). Then
the NCB is resubmitted to NetBIOS to listen for the next datagram.

Note the two global variables SR and SP. These are both string
pointers that point to the same memory. However, SP is a protected
mode pointer (selector:offset) and SR is a real mode pointer
(segment:offset). When processing the string in protected mode (as is
the case within our PostRoutine procedure), you must use the protected
mode pointer. When submitting the pointer to a real mode API call (as
in the call to ReceiveDatagram), you must pass the real mode pointer.

It is essential that you dispose of any variables allocated with
AllocateWinNCB and GetWindowsPostRoutine when you are finished with
them. Care must be taken when disposing of these routines to ensure
that the events associated with them are really complete. If they are
not, the application will probably generate a UAE (unrecoverable
application error). Failure to dispose of these resource when done
will lead to insufficient real mode memory for other processes or
programs. See the routine NBExitProc in the NBCHATW program for an
example of how to safely ensure that variables are properly shutdown
and disposed.

SHARE
-----
In order to make calls to the certain routines in the SHARE unit, the
unit must be able to allocate memory for use in real mode (memory in
the first megabyte of address space). This real mode memory is a
limited resource. Please see the discussion of real mode memory in the
documentation for the WINDPMI unit.

There is no change to the interface of any routines in the SHARE unit
when using them under TPW.

The routines in the SHARE unit work in standard and 386 Enhanced mode
of Windows as well as DOS DPMI. Windows real mode is not supported.

WINDPMI
-------
The WINDPMI unit is new to B-Tree Filer as of version 5.23. It is used
by other units when they are being compiled under TPW or BP7 DPMI. The
WINDPMI unit interfaces various routines relating to the DOS Protected
Mode Interface (DPMI) API.

The DPMI specification 0.9 is supported by Microsoft Windows 3 and by
the DPMI servers supported by Borland Pascal 7.0. This specification
defines routines that allow programs running in protected mode to
communicate with real mode TSRs and device drivers. The DPMI spec also
provides an API for real mode programs to allocate extended memory and
operate in protected mode.

The WINDPMI unit allows protected mode applications to communicate
with real mode TSRs and device drivers. This is necessary to make
calls to underlying network operating systems such as NetWare,
NetBIOS, and MS-NET redirector calls.

With the exception of one important facility, the routines in the
WINDPMI unit are not documented (beyond the comments in the source).
The one documented facility is discussed below under the heading
Real Mode Memory Hooks.

The WINDPMI unit will compile under TPW or BP7 protected mode only. It
will not compile under DOS versions of Turbo Pascal. While some DPMI
calls make sense for Dos applications, the purpose of this unit is to
provide services to Windows and DPMI applications.

Real Mode Memory Hooks
----------------------
The first megabyte of memory in PC compatible computers is accessible
to any member of the 8086 family of microprocessors in real mode. This
is the mode that MS-DOS/PC-DOS runs under. Starting with the 80286, a
new mode called protected mode is available. In this mode, memory
beyond the first megabyte is addressable. In protected mode, regions
of memory are "protected" by the CPU hardware against unauthorized
access. In other words, only programs that have been granted
sufficient access rights to a region of memory may access it.

When Microsoft Windows is operating in its standard or 386 enhanced
modes, it is using the CPU in protected mode. This poses some problems
when run on top of DOS, which works in real mode. It is beyond the
scope of this text to explain all the issues, but there is one area
where an understanding of the problem is required to make use of the
tools provided in the NETWARE, NETBIOS, and SHARE units.

When a program operating in protected mode needs to make a call to a
service provided by a DOS TSR or device driver operating in real mode,
certain strict rules must be followed. Memory in the PC is addressed
differently for real mode and protected mode. Code executing in real
mode can only access memory within the first megabyte of storage
space. Code executing in protected mode can only access memory for
which it has access permission. This poses some intriguing problems!

The bottom line is that Windows and DPMI provide routines that allow
the allocation of real mode memory, and routines to allow protected
mode programs to be granted permission to access it. The one catch is
that real mode memory is a limited resource. To be able to use it
reliably, you need to ensure that sufficient real mode memory will be
available to a program needing it.

This is where the WINDPMI unit comes in. It interfaces two procedural
parameters: GetRealModeMem and FreeRealModeMem. By default, these
point to routines that use the Windows API calls GlobalDosAlloc and
GlobalDosFree (which are also emulated by Borland's DPMI runtime
manager). Provided that sufficient real mode memory exists at the time
of these calls, the default configuration works fine.

As mentioned before, real mode memory is a scarce resource. There is
nothing keeping Windows from using the little memory there is
available below one megabyte for its own use. The reason for the hooks
in WINDPMI is to facilitate the writing of a DLL to be loaded by
Windows automatically that grabs some chunk of real mode memory and
partitions it out to WINDPMI users as needed.


3.  Conditional Defines and Compiler Directives
=====================================================================
As of version 5.40, only three network-related defines are offered in
BTDEFINE.INC. These are:

  {$DEFINE NoNet}
  {.$DEFINE Novell}
  {.$DEFINE MsNet}

See the following section for additional information.

The defines DebugEMSHeap, NoErrorCheckEMSHeap, and ManualInitEMSHeap
have been moved from BTDEFINE.INC into EMSHEAP.PAS, which is the only
unit they affect.

BTDEFINE.INC defines a new symbol named Btree54. This symbol can be
used by utilities that build on B-Tree Filer to detect the new
behavior of the 5.4 version. The symbol Btree52 is also still defined.

The following defines are defined when appropriate in BTDEFINE.INC and
used internally by units to provide appropriate code for each
supported version of the Turbo Pascal compiler: DPMIOrWnd, Heap6,
CanAllowOverlays, and CanSetOvrflowCheck.


4.  FILER
=====================================================================
As of version 5.40, workstation numbering has been redesigned to be
network independent. There is no longer an IsamWSNr variable; the
workstation number is always determined automatically on a
per-fileblock basis. A maximum of MaxNrOfWorkStations users can open
any given fileblock. Call the new function BTGetInternalDialogID to
get the effective workstation number for a given fileblock. This
dialog ID number is obtained by placing a single byte lock at a large
phantom offset into the DIA file; each workstation locks a unique byte
until MaxNrOfWorkstations all have a lock and no more can be obtained.
Note that this lock remains in place continuously until the fileblock
is closed.

NetSupportType now contains only the values NoNet, Novell, and MsNet.
Similarly, the only defines allowed in BTDEFINE.INC are NoNet, Novell,
and MsNet. These values now determine the behavior of the locking
calls and they have no effect on workstation numbering. NoNet means
that locking is ignored, Novell uses NetWare-specific locks, and MsNet
uses generic DOS locks. It is safe to use MsNet for any PC-based
network, including Novell, although the Novell locking calls are more
efficient.

The algorithm used for automatic locking retries has been improved.
For Novell locking, FILER takes advantage of NetWare services to retry
automatically. For MsNet locking, FILER retries the locks itself. The
IsamRetriesForLock typed constant is now gone. Instead, FILER provides
the typed constant IsamLockTimeOut, which is the total number of
milliseconds to retry any lock (default 768 ms). For fileblock locks,
FILER retries for a longer period, by a factor of
IsamFBLockTimeOutFactor, which defaults to 4 (hence, fileblock locks
are retried automatically for about 3 seconds). The BTIsamLockRecord
function's parameters were changed to pass TimeOut and DelayTime
parameters to control the retry behavior. The IsamDelayBetwLocks typed
constant still specifies the number of milliseconds to delay between
lock attempts. A new bonus program, TRAFFIC, can be used to optimize
these timing parameters for your situation. See TRAFFIC.LZH on the
BONUS disk.

Internal locking behavior for fileblock locks has been changed
slightly to reduce lock thrashing on heavily loaded networks. One side
effect of this change may be important in your applications. In
earlier versions, if one station had a write lock on a fileblock, it
could convert this lock to a readlock (by calling BTReadLockFileBlock)
without checking for errors. This is no longer the case, especially on
Novell networks where server lock queueing is supported.

The new routine BTInformTTSAbortSuccessful replaces the old routine
BTDestroyBufferContents. It is used to inform the FILER unit that a
NetWare TTS abort was completed on a particular fileblock.

The new routines BTGetNextUsedAddRecRef and
BTGetAfterNextUsedAddRecRef return the data reference number of the
next record added and the number of the record added after that,
respectively. If you use these routines in a network setting, be sure
to lock the fileblock before calling them; otherwise the number
returned may be changed by another workstation.

BTDeleteRec now returns error 10135 if an attempt is made to delete a
record larger than the total number of records in the file. It still
returns 10135 if an attempt is made to delete record 0.

The methodology for adding a new network type to FILER is now somewhat
different. However, with the new methodology for determining
"workstation numbers", it is less likely that you'll need to add a new
network type. Call TurboPower for details if you find a need to do
this.

When using FILER fileblocks in concert with Novell TTS (transaction
tracking system), do not mark the dialog file transactional. In some
situations, this prevents a call to TTSEnd from actually ending the
transaction.

A few users of the FILER unit have reported that the DR DOS SHARE
utility does not correctly emulate MS-DOS SHARE. According to these
reports, workstations running DR DOS do not work correctly with B-Tree
Filer when it is running as an MsNet network. If you have any insights
into this issue we would be pleased to hear them.

See the text file named ERRORS. in the Filer directory for an
up-to-date list of the error codes used by the FILER unit.

Here are the declarations of new or changed types, constants, and
procedures in the FILER unit:

const
  MaxNrOfWorkStations : Word = 50;

MaxNrOfWorkStations is now a typed constant and can be modified at
runtime. Its value for a given run of the program _must_ be set
before BTInitIsam is called, and hence must be the same for all the
fileblocks opened by B-Tree Filer. Its value can be between 1 and
65534. Please note that if the value is changed, the dialog files
(whose sizes are dependent on MaxNrOfWorkStations) must be deleted
and rebuilt for each fileblock.

const
  IsamFBLockTimeOutFactor : Word = 4;

Multiplier of IsamLockTimeOut for fileblock locks.

const
  IsamLockTimeOut : Word = 768;

Maximum total time (in milliseconds) spent retrying a read lock or a
record lock. Fileblock locks are retried for up to
IsamFBLockTimeOutFactor*IsamLockTimeOut milliseconds.

const
  TestNetExistance : Boolean = True;

If true, BTInitIsam will check whether the network specified in the
ExpectedNet parameter exists or not; if the network is not found,
BTInitIsam will fail. If this typed constant is false, no check is
made for the network, it is assumed to be present.

const
  InheritFileHandles = False;

This constant determines whether any child processes will inherit the
B-Tree Filer fileblock handles or not. Note that, if true, the child
process will only inherit the file handles, it will not inherit any of
the B-Tree Filer data structures from the parent. The default is
false to comply with the behavior of previous versions of Filer.

type
  NetSupportType = (NoNet, Novell, MsNet);

Supported locking methods.

Declaration
  function BTGetAfterNextUsedAddRecRef(IFBPtr : IsamFileBlockPtr) : LongInt;
Purpose
  Return the reference number that will be assigned to the record
  added after the next record is added.
Description
  This routine is primarily for internal use.
See Also
  BTGetNextUsedAddRecRef

Declaration
  function BTGetInternalDialogID(IFBPtr : IsamFileBlockPtr) : Word;
Purpose
  Return the internal ID number used for network access to this
  fileblock.
Description
  The number returned by this function is analogous to the IsamWSNr
  variable in previous versions of FILER. For NoNet access to a
  fileblock, BTGetInternalDialogID always returns 0. Otherwise, it
  returns a number in the range 1..MaxNrOfWorkstations.
  BTGetInternalDialog may return a different number for each fileblock
  opened by an application. To determine the internal dialog ID, FILER
  places a "phantom lock" at offsets just preceding $7FFFFFFF in the
  fileblock's dialog file. The lock is removed when the fileblock is
  closed.

Declaration
  function BTGetNextUsedAddRecRef(IFBPtr : IsamFileBlockPtr) : LongInt;
Purpose
  Return the reference number that will be assigned to the next record
  added.
Description
  If you use this routines in a network setting, be sure to lock the
  fileblock before calling it and leave the lock in place until the
  record is actually added; otherwise the number returned may be
  changed by another workstation before the record is added.
See Also
  BTGetAfterNextUsedAddRecRef

Declaration
  procedure BTInformTTSAbortSuccessful(IFBPtr : IsamFileBlockPtr);
Purpose
  Clear all buffered internal data and remove all locks from a file
  after a successful NetWare TTS transaction abort.
Description
  If you call TTSAbort from the NETWARE unit and receive a success
  code, you should call BTInformTTSAbortSuccessful for all fileblocks
  to which the transaction applied. This routine removes all locks
  placed on the fileblock by the current workstation and it also marks
  all memory buffers associated with the fileblock as invalid so that
  FILER won't attempt to write them to disk.

Declaration
  function BTIsamLockRecord(Start, Len : LongInt;
                            Handle : Word;
                            TimeOut, DelayTime : Word ) : Boolean;
Purpose
  Place a lock using the current FILER locking model.
Description
  The bytes from offset Start to offset Start+Len-1 of the file with
  handle Handle are locked. The routine retries for up to TimeOut
  milliseconds. After each failing lock attempt, the routine delays
  internally for DelayTime milliseconds. If the lock is placed
  successfully, the routine returns True; otherwise, False.

  If the Novell model is used, BTIsamLockRecord calls NetWare-specific
  locking function that retries internally on the server. If the MsNet
  model is used, the FILER unit itself performs the retries. If NoNet
  is used, BTIsamLockRecord does nothing but return True.


5.  EMSHEAP
=====================================================================
A new define, StdEMSInstCheck, was added to the unit. When this define
is not active, EMSHEAP determines whether EMS memory is available by
looking for a signature in memory near where the int $67 vector
points. When the define is active, EMSHEAP *also* tries to open the
device 'EMMXXXX0'. Some older EMS drivers fail the second test even
though EMS is actually present. StdEMSInstCheck is undefined by
default.

Several defines that were previously in BTDEFINE.INC (DebugEMSHeap,
NoErrorCheckEMSHeap, ManualInitEMSHeap, UseTPEMS, and UseOPEMS) were
moved into EMSHEAP.PAS, which is the only file they affect.


6.  ISAMTOOL
=====================================================================
The WSNrLogIn and WSNrLogOut routines are no longer provided, since a
similar algorithm is built into the FILER unit. However, no log file
is used by FILER; "phantom locks" are used instead.

The declaration of InvertString was changed and a second version
of it was added to support ASCIIZ strings. Here are the new
declarations:

{$IFDEF LengthByteKeys}
  procedure InvertString(var Dest : string;
                         Source : string;
                         MaxLen : byte);
{$ENDIF}
{$IFDEF ASCIIZeroKeys}
  procedure InvertString(var Dest;
                         var Source;
                         MaxLen : Byte);
{$ENDIF}

The routine works the same as before, except that it returns the
result in the Dest parameter instead of a function result.


7.  NETWARE
=====================================================================
Starting with NetWare version 2.2, Novell changed the way that print
spooling works. The change also affects all versions of NetWare 386.
NetWare's print spooling is now oriented towards print queues instead
of network printer numbers. None of the printing functions in the
NETWARE unit work correctly unless you perform a bit of additional
setup on your file server. You must inform NetWare of the equivalence
between a server printer number and a print queue name. You do so by
putting an entry in the file server startup file AUTOEXEC.NCF:

   SET SPOOL 0 to LASER1

This example associates server printer number 0 with a print queue
named LASER1. You must restart the server before the change takes
effect.

You can also print under NetWare by using the new NETPRNQU unit. See
NETPRN.LZH on the BONUS disk.


8.  VREC
=====================================================================
The internal section buffer is now automatically resized by all
routines that use it, if they detect that the buffer is too small. If
there is insufficient memory when VREC attempts to resize the buffer,
the new error code 10040 is stored in IsamError.


9. DBIMPEXP
=====================================================================
As of version 5.41 B-Tree Filer incorporates a unit that facilitates
the conversion of data from dBase III or IV data and memo files to a
Filer format, and vice versa. When converting from a dBase file to a
Filer fileblock, the ability exists to create a Pascal include file
that describes the record format of the Filer fileblock being
created. Please see the documentation text file DBIMPEXP.DOC for more
details.

Please note that the current version does not support Filer variable
length records or dBase index files.


10. BROWSER
=====================================================================
Retry logic was removed from the internal ReadLock procedure. This
change was needed because the FILER unit now automatically retries
read locks and the additional retries lead to excessive delays.

Browse and BrowseAgain return a function result of 2 and a new
IsamError code of 8102 if the user presses <Enter> (or any user exit
key) while the highlight bar is over a record that has just been
deleted by another workstation.

Before version 5.41, Browse would ignore an <Enter> if the key was
pressed while the fileblock was locked by another station. Now Browse
always exits when <Enter> (or any user exit key) is pressed. It
returns an appropriate IsamError code (usually 10397, a lock prevents
the operation) when there is a fileblock lock. Applications must be
prepared to deal with these new errors. See SIMPDEMO and NETDEMO for
examples.


11. MSORT
=====================================================================
Support for Windows and DPMI has been removed from this unit, since it
proved to be unreliable under certain conditions and could not be
fixed in a reasonable way. The MSORTP.LZH archive found in the BONUS
directory contains a new protected mode merge sort unit that is better
suited for this environment. MSORTP can also be used for DOS real mode
applications developed using Borland Pascal 7.0 or Turbo Pascal 7.0.
(It depends on the PChar data type, which is available to DOS
applications only with BP7 or TP7.) MSORTP.LZH contains a doc file and
example programs as well as the unit itself.


12. NUMKEYS
=====================================================================
As of version 5.41, several new functions have been added to enhance
compatibility with the C version of B-Tree Filer. C programmers work
with null-terminated strings and many of the Pascal strings generated
by NUMKEYS functions are not compatible with null-terminated strings
since the Pascal strings contain embedded nulls. As a result, the C
module that corresponds to NUMKEYS uses a different algorithm for
converting numbers to keys; this algorithm guarantees that the result
string never contains embedded nulls.

The following new NUMKEYS functions are provided to convert between
the two string formats:

  function CStyleNumKey(S : string) : string;
    {-Convert Pascal-style numkey S into a C-style numkey,
      compatible with ASCIIZ}

  function PascalStyleNumKey(S : string) : string;
    {-Convert C-style numkey S into a Pascal-style numkey}

Whenever you plan to create a database that is accessible from the C
version of B-Tree Filer you should either 1) avoid using any function
from the NUMKEYS unit or 2) convert the string to C style by calling
CStyleNumKey before storing the string in the index file. Also note
that the C version does not provide functions that correspond to
PackXxxKey or UnpackXxxKey, since their value is limited after the
restriction of no embedded nulls is imposed.

Another new function allows the creation of a descending key using an
algorithm that is compatible with C-style strings:

  function CStyleDescendingKey(S : string; MaxLen : byte) : string;
    {-Convert S to a descending key, using C-style algorithm}

Note that C-style strings are somewhat longer than the corresponding
Pascal-style string, because the C-style string uses only 7 out of
each 8 bits for data storage. The 8th bit is always set to 1 to
guarantee that each byte is non-zero.

As of version 5.41, the following functions were added to NUMKEYS to
complete the set of numeric data types that it supports:

  function ByteToKey(B : Byte) : String1;
    {-Convert a byte to a string}

  function KeyToByte(S : String1) : Byte;
    {-Convert a string to a byte}

The implementation of these functions is trivial in the Pascal
version, but they are added to provide a match to the corresponding C
module, where the implementation is not so trivial.


13. Clarifications on Filer network usage
=====================================================================
The following topics are proving popular on our technical support
line and in our forum on CompuServe. In an attempt to forestall some
of these queries, this section will expand on some B-Tree Filer
network (or multiuser) usage and efficiency topics.


Internal readlocks
------------------
Whenever you call a B-Tree Filer routine that will read the fileblock
(for example BTGetRec, BTNextKey) without placing a full lock or a
readlock on the fileblock first, Filer will place a temporary read-
lock before doing the required action and will release it afterwards.
It needs to do this: the single call to the high level routine may
result in a cascade of internal reads, and Filer has to be sure that
the fileblock will not alter in the meantime. Furthermore Filer will
read the dialog file every time a readlock is placed, to check whether
the fileblock has been changed by another workstation (see the
discussion of the dialog file below).

In general the only effect on your program will be if you are going to
do a series of read operations in a loop: placing a readlock before
starting the loop will result in a faster, more efficient loop
compared with letting Filer place its internal automatic readlocks.


The Dialog file
---------------
In a network environment the dialog file is important. All fileblock
locks are placed on the dialog file, not on the data or index files.
Without going into too much detail, there is a section of the dialog
file for each workstation (or to be precise, each workstation number).
Placing a readlock on a fileblock locks the workstation's section of
the dialog file, placing a full lock will lock _all_ workstation
sections of the dialog file. This dialog section is further used by
other workstations to signal to yours that the fileblock has been
changed - it consists of a set of bit flags which will be set on by
other workstations, and which your workstation will set off. Every
time a lock is placed on the fileblock (either a full lock, or an
explicit readlock or an implicit readlock) Filer will read the
workstation's dialog section and if the bit flags are on, invalidate
its internal index pages (forcing them to be reread) and rewrite its
section with all the flags off.

The size of a dialog file is proportional to MaxNrOfWorkstations and
the number of indexes for the fileblock itself. Hence if you change
either of these values, you must delete the dialog file and let it be
recreated automatically by BTOpenFileBlock. Similarly if you have a
dialog file created in save mode by a single-user application: delete
it and allow it to be recreated. This also means that all Filer
applications that use a fileblock _must_ have the same value for
MaxNrOfWorkstations. Note that opening a fileblock in read-only mode
will _not_ cause the dialog file to be recreated.


Read-Only mode
--------------
When you open a fileblock in read-only mode from a given workstation
in a multiuser application, Filer will not write anything at all to
the fileblock (either to the data, the index or to the dialog files).
This implies that you must consider an efficiency issue whenever other
workstations can write to the fileblock (ie the AllReadOnly parameter
is set false for the call to BTOpenFileBlock). Basically, the dialog
file bit flags for this workstation will _never_ get set off by Filer,
it would after all require writing to the dialog file. Hence, every
single readlock placed by the application (either explicitly with
BTReadLockFileBlock or implicitly by Filer itself) will cause the
index pages currently held in memory to be marked invalid and
therefore force them to be reread from the index file. To improve
efficiency in this situation consider not using read-only mode at all
(so that the dialog bit flags would get reset properly), or setting
the AllReadOnly parameter to true when opening the fileblock (no
locking issues would arise), or using explicit readlocks.


Setting a full lock on a readlocked file
----------------------------------------
The locking algorithm introduced for efficiency reasons on a heavily
loaded network in version 5.40 of B-Tree Filer introduced a slight
problem to be aware of in certain situations. To recap, if you had a
readlock on a fileblock and then wanted to place a full lock, Filer
would have to remove the readlock first and then place the full lock.
(You cannot have the same region of a file locked twice.) This
process would introduce a small window of opportunity for another
workstation to place a full lock before you could. Ordinarily this
would not matter, except if you were following the example pseudocode
for deleting a record that we present in the manual (page 75).

Basically, what this code shows is how to delete a record in a
multiuser environment. It places a readlock, rereads the record,
compares it with its internal version, if they are equal (ie no one
else has altered the record in the meantime) it places a full lock on
the fileblock and deletes the record. Assume now the following
scenario: two individual workstations try and delete the same record
at the same time. They both get to the point where they try and place
the full lock (they've both decided that the record has not changed).
Filer removes the readlock first for each workstation. The first
workstation gets the full lock, the second has to wait (an internal
Filer process). The first station deletes the record and releases
the lock. The second station gets its full lock, and tries to delete
the record again. Result: a corrupted data file.

Our advice in this situation is to ignore the pseudocode example, and
to follow the actual Pascal code shown a couple of pages further on as
it places a full lock before rereading the record rather than just a
readlock. We are considering methods by which we can get round this
limitation, but are loath to return to the previous locking method
which used two internal physical locks for every full lock (not very
efficient in a heavily loaded network).


14. NetWare Shells Warning
=====================================================================
As noted in the manual, certain older NetWare shells have a bug when
reading a block from disk that has a lock (see page 31 in the manual).
Unfortunately, having solved the bug for shell version 3.22, Novell
then reintroduced it with the shells that are for MS-DOS 6.0.

At the time of writing, the shell versions with this bug are 3.31,
3.32 and those less than 3.22.

The workaround is given in the manual: you have to define the compiler
directive LockBeforeRead in the BTDEFINE.INC file, and then recompile
the FILER unit.

The bug occurs as follows: station A places a lock on a record.
Station B then tries to read the record. What should happen is that
the DOS call to read the record should fail with the carry flag set
indicating an error and AX set to error code 5 (access denied).
However, with one of these buggy shells, the carry flag is not being
set, but AX is still set to error 5. Filer views this as the DOS call
being successful and that 5 bytes were read by the call. Filer (in
most cases) will have tried to read more than 5 bytes. It then reports
this situation as being a 10070 error (not enough bytes were read).
Using the directive LockBeforeRead forces Filer to try and lock the
record before reading it. If it is already locked, an access denied
error is generated.


15.  Corrections to the Manual
=====================================================================
p. 266    The typed constant referred to as <UsedErrorMessage> is
          actually named <UseErrorMessage>.

